{"name":"Adolescent Rodent","hex":"#a99681","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"adolescent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or at the age of adolescence; at the stage between being a child and an adult."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who is in adolescence; someone who has reached puberty but is not yet an adult."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/adolescent"},{"term":"rodent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammal of the order Rodentia, characterized by long incisors that grow continuously and are worn down by gnawing.","A person lacking in maturity, social skills, technical competence or intelligence; lamer. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Gnawing; biting; corroding; applied to a destructive variety of cancer or ulcer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rodent"}],"entries":[{"title":"Adolescence","blocks":[{"text":"Adolescence is a transitional stage of human physical and psychological development that generally occurs during the period from puberty to adulthood. Adolescence is usually associated with the teenage years, but its physical, psychological or cultural expressions may begin earlier or end later. Puberty typically begins during preadolescence, particularly in females. Physical growth and cognitive development can extend past the teens. Age provides only a rough marker of adolescence, and scholars have not agreed upon a precise definition. Some definitions start as early as 10 and end as late as 30. The World Health Organization definition officially designates adolescence as the phase of life from ages 10 to 19."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Teens_sharing_a_song.jpg/330px-Teens_sharing_a_song.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adolescence"},{"title":"Rodent","blocks":[{"text":"Rodents are a group of mammals belonging to the order Rodentia characterized by a single pair of continuously growing incisors in each of the upper and lower jaws. Rodents make up about 40% of all mammal species. Rodents are native to all major landmasses except Antarctica and a handful of isolated oceanic islands — though humans have since introduced them to nearly all of these places too. Most rodents are small animals with robust bodies, short limbs, and long tails. They use their sharp incisors to gnaw food, excavate burrows, and defend themselves. Most eat plant material, but some have more varied diets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Hydrochoeris_hydrochaeris_in_Brazil_in_Petr%C3%B3polis%2C_Rio_de_Janeiro%2C_Brazil_09.jpg/330px-Hydrochoeris_hydrochaeris_in_Brazil_in_Petr%C3%B3polis%2C_Rio_de_Janeiro%2C_Brazil_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodent"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViews_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Cubes_teenager.jpg/330px-Cubes_teenager.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Cubes_teenager.jpg/330px-Cubes_teenager.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Upper body of a teenage boy. The structure has changed to resemble an adult for